 # Octree-based structures
 
-Also called: Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVO), Voxel Hierarchies
+Also called: Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVO), VDB, Tree64, Implicit Octrees
 
 ## Overview
-Octree-based structures are hierarchical data structures that recursively subdivide a 3D domain into eight sub-volumes (octants) to represent sparse volumetric data. They solve the problem of excessive memory usage in dense voxel grids by storing only occupied regions, enabling efficient culling of empty space during rendering or query operations. What sets them apart from sibling methods like Pointer Trees (variable branching factor) and Sparse Voxel DAGs (deduplication) is the fixed 8-way branching factor and the specific trade-offs between pointer overhead, traversal depth, and spatial locality management [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Octree-based structures represent 3D volumetric data using a recursive spatial subdivision where each node has eight children, enabling efficient storage of sparse data by omitting empty regions [S1, unofficial][S2]. They differ from sibling structures like Pointer Trees (variable branching factor) and Sparse Voxel DAGs (subtree merging) by strictly maintaining a fixed 8-way hierarchical topology, which simplifies traversal logic and coordinate mapping at the cost of potentially higher memory overhead for highly redundant geometries compared to DAGs [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
